<dialog class="muzeum-guide" aria-labelledby="muzeum-guide-title" data-guide-target="drawer" data-action="click->guide#dismissOutside close->guide#closed">
  <div class="muzeum-guide-heading" id="guide-overview" tabindex="-1">
    <h2 id="muzeum-guide-title">Muzeum Guide</h2>
    <button type="button" data-action="guide#close" autofocus>Close</button>
  </div>
  <nav aria-label="Guide contents">
    <ul>
      <li><a data-action="guide#navigate" href="#guide-colors">Musical identity colors</a></li>
      <li><a data-action="guide#navigate" href="#guide-releases">Canonical releases</a></li>
      <li><a data-action="guide#navigate" href="#guide-listening">Listening history</a></li>
    </ul>
  </nav>
  <% if controller_name == "canonical_library" && action_name == "show" %>
    <%= render "shared/identity_assignment", entry: @entry, decision: musical_identity(@entry) %>
  <% end %>
  <section aria-labelledby="guide-colors-heading" tabindex="-1" id="guide-colors">
    <h3 id="guide-colors-heading">Musical identity colors</h3>
    <p>The palette describes musical affinities. A two-color badge expresses two meaningful affinities without splitting listening time between them. A release or recording with no colors of its own can inherit a credited artist’s colors. If credited artists have conflicting colors, it stays neutral until you assign its own colors.</p>
    <figure class="guide-dual-example">
      <span aria-hidden="true" class="identity-swatch identity-swatch-dual identity-light-green"></span>
      <figcaption>Jazz and soul, R&amp;B, and funk. These colors describe a musical combination.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <ul class="guide-palette">
      <% MusicalIdentity::Palette::COLORS.each do |color, description| %>
        <li><span aria-hidden="true" class="identity-swatch identity-<%= color %>"></span><%= description %></li>
      <% end %>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="guide-release-heading" tabindex="-1" id="guide-releases">
    <h3 id="guide-release-heading">Canonical releases</h3>
    <p>Muzeum preserves the original Last.fm strings. A confirmed recording can belong to several verified releases. Tracklists document where a recording appears. Which commercial edition was played remains unknown.</p>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="guide-listening-heading" tabindex="-1" id="guide-listening">
    <h3 id="guide-listening-heading">Listening history</h3>
    <p>Muzeum estimates listening time from known recording durations. Plays with unknown durations still contribute to play counts. Combined histories count each observation once, including when releases overlap.</p>
  </section>
</dialog>
